course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 25
title: What time is it? Days, weeks, seasons
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: To meet Sashko in the park you ask "o kotriy?", at what time? — and he says "o p'yatiy", at five.
explain: The days of the week hide a secret: nedilya, Sunday, means "no work"; ponedilok, Monday, is the day after nedilya; vivtorok is the second day, sereda the middle, chetver the fourth, p'yatnytsya the fifth, and subota is the Sabbath.
explain: Then the little time words every kid shouts: "vzhe?" (already?), "shche ni" (not yet), "ty dovho!" (you took ages!), "ty zavzhdy vyhraiesh!" (you always win!).
explain: The year, rik, has four seasons: vesna (spring), lito (summer), osin (autumn) and zyma (winter); and when it's pIzno, late, it's DOdomu.
fact: "o kotriy?" means "at what time?", used when arranging to meet, and the answer is like "o p'yatiy", at five.
fact: "khvylyna" means a minute; "odnu khvylynku!" means "one moment!", and "khvylka" is a short, casual form.
fact: "tyzhden" means a week.
fact: The days of the week are ponedilok, vivtorok, sereda, chetver, p'yatnytsya, subota and nedilya, Monday to Sunday.
fact: "nedilya", Sunday, literally means "no work", and "ponedilok", Monday, is the day after nedilya.
fact: "vykhidni" means the weekend, as in "shcho robysh na vykhidnykh?", what are you doing at the weekend?
fact: "ranO" means early, and "pIzno" means late; "vzhe pIzno, DOdomu" means "it's late already, home".
fact: "vzhe" means already, and "vzhe?" means "already?".
fact: "shche ni" means "not yet": "ty poyiv?" — did you eat? — "shche ni".
fact: "zavzhdy" means always and "nikoly" means never: "ty zavzhdy vyhraiesh!" means "you always win!".
fact: "dovho" means a long time, and "ty dovho!" means "you took ages!".
fact: "rik" means a year, used for age and in "z NOvym rOkom", Happy New Year; it sounds like "reek" but has nothing to do with smells.
fact: "lito", "zyma", "vesna" and "osin" mean summer, winter, spring and autumn.
exercise: You arrange to meet Sashko. How do you ask "at what time?" | o kotriy? | kotra hodyna? | SKILky? | yaka pohOda?
exercise: Sashko says "odnu khvylynku!". What does he mean? | One moment! | One more goal! | One o'clock! | One week!
exercise: How long is a "tyzhden"? | A week | A day | A month | A year
exercise: Which day is "subota"? | Saturday | Sunday | Monday | Friday
exercise: Which day is "ponedilok"? | Monday | Tuesday | Sunday | Wednesday
exercise: What does "nedilya" literally mean? | No work | First day | Holy day | Market day
exercise: Sashko asks "shcho robysh na vykhidnykh?". When is he asking about? | The weekend | Tonight | Tomorrow morning | The holidays
exercise: Sashko's mum says "vzhe pIzno". What does she mean? | It's late already | It's early | It's raining already | It's too cold
exercise: Sashko gets up at 6 on Saturday and his dad says "ranO!". What does he mean? | Early! | Late! | Hurry! | Hungry!
exercise: Sashko finishes his homework in five minutes. His mum says "vzhe?". What does she mean? | Already? | Not yet? | Again? | Why?
exercise: Sashko's mum asks "ty poyiv?" and he hasn't eaten. What does he answer? | shche ni | vzhe | zavzhdy | ranO
exercise: Sashko loses again and moans "ty zavzhdy vyhraiesh!". What does he mean? | You always win! | You never win! | You won today! | You cheat!
exercise: What is the opposite of "zavzhdy"? | nikoly | vzhe | potIm | dovho
exercise: You take ages tying your laces and Sashko groans "ty dovho!". What does he mean? | You took ages! | You're too tall! | You're too early! | You're lost!
exercise: How long is a "rik"? | A year | A week | A day | An hour
exercise: Which word is summer? | lito | zyma | vesna | osin
exercise: Which season is "osin"? | Autumn | Spring | Winter | Summer
test: my mate says "rik" means a smell cos it sounds like "reek". right? | No, rik means a year | Yes, it means a bad smell | Yes, it means smoke | No, it means a week
test: If "ponedilok" is the day after "nedilya", and nedilya means "no work", what does that tell you about Monday? | It is the first work day after the rest day | It is the rest day itself | It is the middle of the week | It is the last day of the week
test: Sashko says "o p'yatiy" when you ask "o kotriy?". When do you meet? | At five | In five minutes | On day five | On Friday
test: If "sereda" means the middle, why does Wednesday fit? | It is the middle of a Monday-to-Sunday week | It is the first day | It is the weekend | It is the day of rest
test: "vivtorok" sounds like "second" and "chetver" like "fourth". Counting from Monday, which days are they? | Tuesday and Thursday | Monday and Wednesday | Wednesday and Friday | Saturday and Sunday
test: crap, it's "pIzno" and you're still at Sashko's. What will his mum most likely say? | vzhe pIzno, DOdomu | ranO, hrAimo! | shche ni | vykhidni!
test: Sashko asks if you've done your homework and you haven't. What's the honest short answer? | shche ni | vzhe | zavzhdy | nikoly ne
test: Your mate says "lito" is winter because "it's a little season". What's lito? | Summer | Winter | Spring | Autumn
test: Sashko wants a lie-in "na vykhidnykh". Which days does he mean? | subota and nedilya | ponedilok and vivtorok | sereda and chetver | p'yatnytsya only
test: What would happen if you told Sashko "ty nikoly ne vyhraiesh!" just after he won? | It would be untrue: he just won, and "nikoly" means never | It means "well done!" | It means "you always win!" | It means "play again"
test: Sashko's mum says "odnu khvylynku" on the phone and talks for twenty minutes. What did she promise? | One moment | Twenty minutes | One hour | Tomorrow
exam: Ascertain the literal signification of the Ukrainian noun "nedilya", designating the seventh day of the week. | "No work", that is, a day of rest | "First day", that is, the commencement of the week | "Market day", that is, a day of trade | "Middle day", that is, the midpoint of the week
exam: It is given that "ponedilok" denotes the day following "nedilya". Determine which day of the week is thereby designated. | Monday | Sunday | Saturday | Tuesday
exam: Identify the enquiry appropriate to the fixing of the hour of a prospective meeting. | o kotriy? | kotra hodyna? | yak teBE zvut? | shcho NOvoho?
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the terms "zavzhdy" and "nikoly". | The former signifies "always" and the latter "never" | The former signifies "never" and the latter "always" | Both signify "already" | Both signify "later"
